  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high that quiet and dry conditions will\npersist through late Saturday under high pressure.\n\nRain chances and gusty winds will increase late Saturday night\ninto Sunday as Hurricane Isaias tracks northward over the next\n72 hours. Although the system is expected to weaken as it moves\ninland towards the Appalachian Mountains, it will likely \nmaintain a closed low-pressure circulation south of Ohio and in \nnorthern Kentucky; remaining a tropical depression into Sunday.\nThe intense wind field aloft (40-45kts at 850mb), combined with\nincreasing low-level moisture and warm air advection, will\nsupport periods of moderate to heavy rainfall within passing\nbands early Sunday morning and afternoon. \n\nFlooding concerns are expected to remain low following several\ndays of dry weather and the probability of 6 hour rainfall\namounts exceeding flash flood guidance remains low (less than \n10%). However, localized ponding and clogged storm drains, \nparticularly due to falling leaves, could pose minor issues in \nsome neighborhoods. Rainfall totals of 0.50 to 1.25 inches are \nexpected across the region over a 24-hour period. Elevated\ninstability (shallow but noted) could prompt a few lightning\nstrikes on Sunday. Otherwise, the probability of severe storms\nis considered low. \n\nRainfall intensity is expected to decrease Sunday afternoon and\nevening as dry slot advances into the region from the southwest.\nIsolated light rain showers or drizzle may linger into early\nMonday as the closed low opens into a wave and quickly tracks\neastward. \n\nA secondary concern with this system will be the strong wind\ngusts reaching the surface, particularly along the ridges, where\ngusts of 30-45mph are possible. A tight pressure gradient and\nthe potential for downslope flow, as low-level winds align\nperpendicular to the ridges axes, could enhanced gusts at times.\nElsewhere, much of the strong low-level wind field is expected \nto remain aloft beneath a pronounced inversion, supported by \npersistent warm air advection. \n\nQuiet and drier weather is expected late Monday morning into \nTuesday as high pressure returns. A passing cold front late\nTuesday returns light rain chances to the region.",
